<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The background layer of the adaptive launcher icon. Chapter 30.

    THIS FILE EXISTS BECAUSE THE ICON WAS INVISIBLE.

    An adaptive icon is two layers: this one, and `ic_launcher_foreground`, which
    the launcher masks to whatever shape the device uses. The foreground is
    correctly a white sparkle on transparency — it must be, because it is the
    layer that gets masked and parallaxed, and baking a background into it would
    show as a square corner poking out of a circular mask.

    Capacitor's scaffold left the background as a flat `#FFFFFF` colour. White
    mark, white background: the app drawer rendered CartWise as an empty white
    circle. That is what the emulator screenshot showed, and no amount of
    checking the generated PNGs would have found it, because every one of those
    PNGs was correct on its own.

    So the gradient lives here, on the layer that is allowed to have one.

    WHY A SHAPE DRAWABLE AND NOT THE GENERATED PNG

    A PNG would have to exist at five densities at 108dp for a flat wash, and it
    would be a fourth copy of the brand colours to keep in step. A shape scales
    to any density from six lines and names the two hex values once.

    The angle is 315 rather than 45: Android measures gradient angle
    anticlockwise from east, so 315 runs top-left to bottom-right and matches
    both the web header's `bg-gradient-to-br` and the store assets that
    tools/generate-app-icons.ps1 draws at GDI+'s 45 (which measures the other
    way). The two numbers differ; the direction on screen is the same.
-->
<shape xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="rectangle">
    <gradient
        android:type="linear"
        android:angle="315"
        android:startColor="#2563EB"
        android:endColor="#7C3AED" />
</shape>
